## 2. Building the Static Skeleton First `[7:03 – 12:18]`

### Why Start with Static HTML?
**Teaching technique used throughout this lecture:** the instructor first writes **plain, non-functional HTML** (hardcoded tasks, non-working buttons) so you can see the *visual layout* clearly, **before** wiring up any Vue logic. This separates "how it should look" from "how it should behave" — a good practice when building any UI.

### 2.1 The Heading

```html
<div id="app">
  <h2>Task Manager</h2>
  <h3>My Daily Tasks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Task 1</li>
    <li>Task 2</li>
  </ul>
</div>
```

- `<h2>` for the app title, `<h3>` for a sub-heading ("My Daily Tasks") — pure HTML heading levels controlling font size/prominence (nothing Vue-specific).
- `<ul>` (unordered list) + `<li>` (list item) — used to statically display two dummy tasks, just to visualize the layout.

> 💡 At this point, this is **just decoration** — you cannot add/remove tasks by interacting with the page; you'd have to manually edit the HTML. That's exactly the limitation Vue will solve.

### 2.2 The Input Form (Initially Built the "Old" HTML Way)

```html
<form>
  <label for="myTask">Enter your task</label>
  <input type="text" id="myTask" name="task">
  <input type="submit" value="Add Task">
</form>
```

**Why did the instructor build this using a `<form>` first?** To contrast the **old/traditional way** (used in earlier "MAD 1"-style apps with Flask/Jinja backends) with the **Vue way** — an important conceptual bridge.

#### Q&A: What's the difference between `<form>` and `<input>`?
- `<form>` = a **container** that groups related inputs together (text fields, passwords, submit buttons, etc.).
- When a form is submitted (e.g., by clicking a `type="submit"` button), the **browser packages all the input values inside that form** and sends them somewhere (traditionally, to a backend server) — this packaged data is called the **payload**.
- The `name` attribute on each `<input>` is critical in this traditional flow, because it becomes the **key** under which that input's value is stored in the payload (e.g., `name="task"` → the value gets sent as `task=<whatever was typed>`).

**Why is none of this needed in a Vue app?**
> Because Vue apps handle everything with **JavaScript directly in the browser** — there's no traditional form submission happening. Instead of the browser packaging data and sending it to a backend endpoint, Vue directly captures input values into your `data` object in real time (via `v-model`, covered in Section 4). So:
> - We **don't need** the `<form>` wrapper at all.
> - We **don't need** the `name` attribute.
> - We **don't need** `type="submit"` — we'll use a regular `<button>` instead.

### 2.3 Removing the Form, Simplifying to Plain Elements

```html
<label for="myTask">Enter your task</label>
<input type="text" id="myTask">
<button>Add</button>
```

**Proof this matters:** With the old `<form>` + `type="submit"` setup, clicking "Add" caused the **entire page to reload** (default browser behavior for form submission). After removing the `<form>` wrapper and using a plain `<button>`, clicking does **nothing** (yet) — no reload — which is exactly the foundation needed for Vue to take over with JavaScript-driven behavior instead.

### 📝 Remember This — Section 2
| Old (traditional HTML form) way | New (Vue) way |
|---|---|
| `<form>` wraps inputs | No `<form>` needed |
| `name="..."` attribute stores payload key | Not needed — `v-model` handles capturing values |
| `<input type="submit">` | Plain `<button>` |
| Clicking submit reloads/redirects the page | Clicking a Vue button triggers JS logic with **no reload** |

### ✅ Section 2 Summary
- Static HTML was built first to visualize the layout: a heading, a hardcoded list, and a (traditional) form.
- The traditional `<form>`/`name`/`submit` pattern was then intentionally stripped away, because Vue apps don't submit data to a backend this way — they manage everything as JavaScript state in the browser.

### 🎯 Practice Questions
1. Why is the `name` attribute unnecessary in a Vue-driven form?
2. What browser behavior changes when you remove the `<form>` wrapper and use a plain `<button>` instead of `type="submit"`?
3. What is a "payload" in the context of traditional HTML forms?

