## 4. Capturing New Task Input with `v-model` `[32:21 – 35:32]`

```javascript
data: {
  myTasks: [],
  task: ''    // will temporarily hold whatever the user is typing
}
```

```html
<input type="text" v-model="task">
```

- `v-model="task"` links this input box to the `task` data property — **two-way binding**, exactly as covered last session.
- Whatever the user types is immediately available as `this.task` inside the script, and can be displayed anywhere in the HTML via `{{ task }}`.

**Quick demo used by instructor:**
```html
<p>Current task: {{ task }}</p>
```
Typing into the input instantly updates this paragraph — proving the two-way binding is live and working, before moving on to actually *using* this value meaningfully (adding it to the list).

### 📝 Remember This — Section 4
- `v-model` = the bridge between what the user types and your `data`.
- This captured value (`task`) is temporary — it holds "whatever is currently in the input box," not the final list of tasks.

