## 15. Full-Session "Remember This" — Exam-Focused Recap

- **Building a real app** requires combining `el`, `data`, `v-for`, `v-if`/`v-else`, `v-model`, `v-on`/`methods`, `computed`, and `watch` — each solving a different kind of problem.
- **`methods`** → explicit, user-triggered actions (clicking Add, clicking Mark Complete).
- **`computed`** → automatically-derived values from existing data, re-displayed as if they were data themselves (completed/pending task counts).
- **`watch`** → reacts to a specific data property changing, in order to trigger *independent* side effects (live validation messages, dynamic styling flags) — the key differentiator from `computed` is that watch's output need not be "the same information in different form"; it can affect entirely separate parts of the app's state.
- **Objects vs. strings in arrays:** Use objects when each item needs multiple related properties (id, name, status, etc.).
- **JavaScript array methods to know:** `.push()` (add to end), `.find()` (first match), `.filter()` (all matches), `.length` (count).
- **Reference semantics:** Assigning an object/array to a new variable does not copy it — both variables point to the same data, so mutating one affects the other (and, crucially, this is *why* Vue's reactivity picks up the change).
- **`v-bind:class="{ className: booleanFlag }"`** dynamically toggles CSS classes based on data — a natural extension of "UI is a function of state" into the visual styling domain.
- **No traditional `<form>`/`name`/`submit` needed** in Vue apps — `v-model` + `methods` fully replace that older pattern for client-side-only interactivity.

